After clearing the game 3 times I can finally give my two cents about the game.
TLDR; version
If you like games in which your instincts are put to test, then this game is for you. I have mixed feeling about recommending this to souls fans has the board is split between those who like The Surge and those who spit on it for various reasons.
With that said I find the game good, but a little too short, and hoped for more changes from NG+ onward. Still a great game though!
Full Version:
You play as Warren (a premade MALE character, no character creator for you, so either stick with it or avoid the game), who gets his surgery botched and find himself in a facility which goes crazy and it's up to you to uncover the truth behind all that.
The gameplay in The Surge is SIMILAR to souls games with a few differences:
- Character progression is Implant based: instead of levelling up, you equip "items" to increase your health, stamina, and so on;
- The game is not about evasion only: many people fail to understand that you are not supposed to only evade attacks, but you can block and parry many of them aswell. This is CRUCIAL to lower the difficulty the enemies pose to you;
- There is a weapon profiency system: not so many players like it, as they say that it forces you to stick with a single weapon type for the entire playthrough. I disagree with this, simply because the damage boost is minimal, and you need a VERY HIGH level of profiency in order to see a massive boost in that regard;
- Bosses drops 2 versions of their weapons: I read somewhere that Lord of The Fallen had a similar system aswell, but I can't vouch it as I never played that game. There are some hidden requirements in order to get the better version of bosses weapons (named as 2.0), and while "scary" you can easily find any information about them anywhere;
- The game main focus are the trash mobs, instead of the bosses: indeed, the game difficulty is focused more on trash mobs, with them dealing TONS of damage;
- The combat system: I find it to be beautiful and very fluid. There are many combo which you can make, but don't get too greedy;
- NG+ and more: the game features NG+ with increasing difficulty per plus INDEFINITELY, meaning the game difficulty will always increase, while your gear's upgrade have their limits (this could make progression impossibile at some points, at least this is what the devs said).
As any game, there are some good and bad points, which I'm going to summarize right now:
- A difficult game nonetheless;
- Weapons and armor sets are eye candy;
- Interesting story and concept (albeit it leaves many questions);
- Levels well connected and well designed (for my tastes, at least);
- NG+!
- Easy to learn, hard to master;
- Preorder DLC available after launch (this is more of a personal thing, but I'm happy to see this practice instead of some ♥♥♥♥♥♥ limited time only dlc).
- The game is a bit short. Sure, there is NG+ but you end up doing the same thing over and over;
- Enemies deal a TON of damage. I agree that this is not a soul game and it must differ in some ways from that franchise, but it feels stupid when you wear a full plated armor which makes you look like a mobile fortress (and badass) only to be one shotted by one trash mob's lariat you didn't see because of different reasons (due to camera, or the enemy was hidden and so on). It's not a problem for me, as the game isn't that difficult once you master it, but still it feels out of place;
- Some weapons feature a 2-3 hit attacks per single button pressed. While I have no issue with this at all (as it's a matter of memorizing x weapon and get to know the right time to unleash these attacks), many people don't like this system at all apparently, so take it as a small warning;
- There are few weapons and armor. Weapons are split in 5 different types, and...that's all. Armors aren't that many aswell;
- Did I already said the game is a bit short?? If you enjoyed the game, you will want more. I really like to play this, but I really hoped for way more content.
With all of the above said, I greatly reccomend this game to anyone who likes no-holding hand games.
There is only one thing left to say: PRAISE THE SURGE!

